<template>
    <children>
        <li id="34.lp5"/>
        <li id="33.lp5"/>
        <li id="32.lp5"/>
    </children>
    <heading>Model</heading>
    <keywords>
        <li>model</li>
        <li>project</li>
        <li>directory handle</li>
        <li>IndexedDB</li>
    </keywords>
    <section data-lp5-kind="explanation">
        <p>The model keeps one authoritative article representation for navigation, code expansion, and future editing. Article records retain detached XML documents: the <a href="#31.lp5">view</a> may render the same source in different ways without making rendered HTML the project data. The maps below share those records rather than maintaining separate article copies.</p>
        <p>Consumers subscribe with <code>model.addEventListener("ProjectLoaded", listener, options?)</code> and unsubscribe with <code>model.removeEventListener</code>, using the standard EventTarget arguments. A private target separates model notifications from unrelated document events. Each successful load or reload emits one synchronous <code>ProjectLoaded</code> event after all articles and indexes are installed and before the load Promise resolves. Listeners read the complete model through its query interface; the event carries no copied article data or storage handle. Its <code>detail.selectionId</code> distinguishes selection lifetimes: reloading keeps the ID, and selecting a different opened storage object changes it. This lets views retain per-selection state without comparing project names or accessing storage. Reopening the same location as a new storage object counts as a new selection. Selection alone and failed loads emit no completion event. Subscriptions survive replacement of the article data.</p>
        <p>File access is supplied as an opened <a href="#35.lp5">storage object</a>, so the same loader can work with a local directory or an HTTP project. Selection and loading are separate calls, serialized by the <a href="#21.lp5">operation</a>; choosing a storage location alone does not replace the loaded articles. <a href="#28.lp5">Picker activation</a>, <a href="#30.lp5">remembered handles</a>, and <a href="#31.lp5">UI lifetime</a> belong to the selecting operation and its helpers.</p>
